\description{
Performs selection-adjusted inference for the harm subgroup discovered
by \code{dina_subgroup()}.  For each of \code{n_boot} bootstrap resamples of the
input data frame, the function refits DINA via \code{dina()} and re-runs
the subgroup search via \code{dina_subgroup()}, then reports three classes
of output:
}
\details{
\itemize{
\item An \strong{effect CI} on \verb{a*^T beta_b}, where \verb{a* = (1, x_bar_S)} is
held FIXED from the original-data subgroup \verb{S*} and \code{beta_b} is
each iteration's DINA coefficient vector.  This is the
bootstrap analog of the Wald CI returned by
\code{dina_subgroup()} -- conditional on the discovered subgroup
definition, with width reflecting sampling variance of
\code{beta-hat}.
\item \strong{Stability CIs} on the bootstrap-selected \code{n_subgroup} and the
cut threshold(s), restricted to iterations that selected the same
cut-set (covariate(s) and direction(s)) as the original-data
subgroup.  For a depth-2 conjunction the threshold CI is reported
per covariate.
\item A \strong{selection-frequency table} counting how often each cut-set
was chosen.  The structural-selection stability diagnostic.
}

Each bootstrap iteration calls \code{dina()} (single-pass, sandwich
variance) under the hood, not \code{dina_bagged()}.

If you have already run \code{dina()} and \code{dina_subgroup()} on the original
data, pass those objects via \code{fit} and/or \code{sg} so the original-data
point estimate reuses them rather than being recomputed here.  This
guarantees the reported point estimate matches your standalone result
exactly; otherwise the internal refit uses its own cross-fitting fold
assignment, which at a boundary-case \code{m_diff} can disagree with the
upstream fit on whether a subgroup qualifies.
}
\section{Why the bootstrap target is the \emph{fixed-subgroup} effect}{

A naive bootstrap of \code{dina_subgroup()}'s \code{mean_tau_hat} is
uninformative.  Under the default \code{sg_focus = "maxSG"} the search
selects the \emph{largest} subgroup whose mean tau-hat exceeds \code{m_diff},
so the chosen subgroup lies at the boundary by construction: adding
one more patient would push the mean below \code{m_diff}.  Each bootstrap
iteration's selected subgroup is similarly pinned, so the bootstrap
distribution of \code{mean_tau_hat} clusters tightly above \code{m_diff}
and the resulting CI quantifies threshold-grid granularity
rather than effect uncertainty.  (Under the band foci
\code{"effMaxSG"} / \code{"effMinSG"} the per-iteration subgroup is instead
anchored to the maximum effect rather than the \code{m_diff} boundary,
but the same caveat applies: \code{mean_tau_hat} is a selection-pinned
quantity, not an effect estimate.)  The fixed-subgroup linear
functional \verb{a*^T beta_b} instead targets the clinically
meaningful estimand "treatment effect for patients whose
covariates lie in the discovered subgroup".  Because DINA's
\code{beta-hat} is cross-fit (and hence asymptotically unbiased even
for data-driven contrasts), the percentile distribution of
\verb{a*^T beta_b} gives a non-parametric CI that complements the
sandwich Wald CI of \code{dina_subgroup()}.
}

\section{Two complementary effect estimates}{

When \code{refit = TRUE} (default), the function reports two treatment
effects for the discovered subgroup, on the same natural-parameter
scale:
\itemize{
\item the \strong{DINA effect} (\code{effect_ci}): the BLP-analog
\verb{a*^T beta}, a projection of the cross-fitted CATE surface
averaged over the subgroup; and
\item the \strong{within-subgroup standard-model effect}
(\code{refit_effect_ci}): the treatment coefficient from a plain Cox
model (or GLM) fit on the subgroup, refit within the FIXED
discovered signature on each resample.  This is the contrast a
clinical-trial reader usually expects.
}
The two coincide only under correct linearity; reporting both is
informative.  Crucially, \emph{both} CIs are conditional on the
discovered signature treated as pre-specified -- neither corrects for
the data-driven selection of the signature.  A selection-adjusted
(de-biased) interval requires the more involved bias-corrected
bootstrap of Leon et al. (2024, Section 3.2) and is not provided
here; selection \emph{stability} is instead summarized by
\code{selection_frequency}.
}

\section{Performance}{

With \code{parallel = "boots"}, each bootstrap iteration runs in a worker
via \verb{foreach::foreach() \%dofuture\%}.  Set \code{future::plan()} to a
parallel backend (e.g., \code{future::multisession}) before calling, and
reset to \code{"sequential"} afterwards.  Workers run from the installed
package; \code{devtools::install()} is required after code edits before
the parallel branch will see them.
}

\examples{
\dontrun{
set.seed(1)
n <- 400
df_demo <- data.frame(
  w  = stats::rbinom(n, 1, 0.5),
  x1 = stats::runif(n, -1, 1),
  x2 = stats::runif(n, -1, 1)
)
tau_x      <- 0.4 + 1.2 * df_demo$x1
df_demo$y  <- 0.5 * df_demo$x1 + df_demo$w * tau_x + stats::rnorm(n)

bs <- dina_subgroup_bootstrap(
  df         = df_demo,
  outcome    = "y",
  treatment  = "w",
  covariates = c("x1", "x2"),
  family     = "gaussian",
  m_diff     = 0.5,
  n_boot     = 50L,
  seed       = 1L
)
print(bs)
}

}
